Separate thermodynamic feasibility from evidence for a process (@physics-entropy-evidence: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Investigate-entropy-work-bounds-and-changing-source-temperatures.lingua#r_0099FE8B08ED994548C5F84AD2") { r_0017AF3D433C65A9E9AAB740EB
An energy balance, entropy screen and constitutive model answer different questions. Energy closure checks what crosses a chosen boundary and what is stored. Entropy constrains process direction under the macroscopic law. A material model connects measured state quantities to U and S. Passing one does not establish the others. A proposed negative entropy generation may indicate an impossible proposal under the model, a missing exchange, inconsistent signs, measurement error or an invalid property relation; investigate which claim the evidence supports.

For the gas expansions, equal endpoint T and V imply equal ΔS within the ideal-gas model but allow different Q,W and generation. For the finite bodies, final temperature and capacity information determine the predicted work only if other exchanges/storage are excluded. Measurements must define equilibrium endpoints, temperature scales, gas amount or heat capacities, load/work boundaries and relevant heat transfers. Repeatability of a temperature reading does not establish constant capacity or an ideal-gas law.

The boundary temperature in an entropy-transfer integral is the temperature at the selected heat-transfer surface. It may match a reservoir or uniform system temperature in an appropriate limit. It is not necessarily equal to either in an actual finite-gradient transfer. Avoid a blanket inequality between boundary and system temperature as well as an unjustified equality. Moving the boundary can change the allocation of generated entropy while preserving a correctly assembled composite total.

**Published numerical reconstruction:** [Introduction to Engineering Thermodynamics — Claire Yu Yan](../thermodynamics-and-heat-transfer/sources/Introduction-to-Engineering-Thermodynamics-Yan.lingua#r_6AX93SRMGE0XXJTQ1NW6X9QXTW), section 6.7.3 Example 3, uses air as an ideal gas with constant c_p=1.005 kJ/(kg K), R=0.287 kJ/(kg K), initial 100 kPa and 27 °C, final 600 kPa and 67 °C. Converting to 300.15 K and 340.15 K gives Δs=1.005 ln(340.15/300.15)−0.287 ln6≈−0.38850509884 kJ/(kg K), which rounds to the published −0.3885. The negative system change does not by itself violate the second law; heat transfer and surrounding changes are required to determine generation. This reconstructs the supplied property-model calculation, not a compression experiment or independent air-property measurement.

**Critique and communicate:** write one table separating supplied data, constitutive assumptions, derived state changes, actual path information and unmeasured terms. Distinguish a reversible comparison from a claim that a process was performed reversibly. Explain the difference between maximum efficiency and maximum power in the source reading. Reuse [Report uncertainty with its interpretation and decision boundary](../../inquiry/core/Report-uncertainty-with-its-interpretation-and-decision-boundary.lingua#r_00C8CF675ED315159661399E79) and [Review fitting evidence before extending scientific claims](../../inquiry/core/Review fitting evidence before extending scientific claims.lingua#r_008E6FD7D2B90DB8ED1CE9927E) when deciding what an observation or fitted property supports.

Statistical entropy, microscopic state counting, variable-property and phase-change relations, and general nonequilibrium transport require further preparation. They remain in the physics formation; this macroscopic block does not replace them with an informal disorder analogy.

